# Online Network Database Manager

How the world (`research/world.dj`) is made durable. Data persistence only: the
file, and the rules that keep it readable.

## What we specify

- *One file, mapped.* `MAP_SHARED` on a btrfs file. The mapped region is memory
  and can be paged off partially; references are offsets, not addresses, because
  the mapping lands somewhere new each run.
- *Framed and checksummed per unit*, so a unit's extent and integrity are knowable
  without reading what precedes it.
- *Checked at open.* Decode every unit into the type when the store opens. An
  unknown tag, version or payload type *aborts* the open; a failed checksum is
  *damage*, and the unit is skipped and reported.
- *A mutation publishes with a single store*, from a power-safe overwrite: the
  store assumes an aligned store lands entirely or not at all, so a new payload is
  written elsewhere and published with one store, and a variable-length record is
  never rewritten in place.
- *Compaction only at the edges*, never in the middle of a run.

## Prior art

- `research/persistence-protobuf.dj` — the record that says what it is; gives the
  record, not the store.
- `research/persistence-pmdk.dj` — Intel's PMDK, the closest prior art, dead.
- `research/persistence-object-stores.dj` — the persistent-object-store line, and
  where the offset-pointer, publish and check-at-open mechanisms come from.

## The rest — the principles behind the constraints, and what is open

None of these is a device guarantee; they are the reasoning above the rules, kept
last.

*The kernel never crashes.* A `write(2)` that returns has its bytes in the page
cache, and the kernel writes them out, so process-crash durability needs no
`fsync`. What remains is the process dying mid-instruction, so the format has to
tolerate a torn tail — a formatting problem, not a durability one.

*Corruption is local.* A corrupted unit must not take the rest with it: frame and
checksum every unit, keep no whole-file index, truncate the tail on open, and
recover per unit — skip a damaged unit and report it, never fail the file. The
pairing that makes this cheap: no `fsync` and per-unit checksums give up the
guarantee between writes and get it back per record.

*Persistent and in-memory are one thing.* The world (`research/world.dj`) is the
model and the file is a rendering of it; reads are pointer-chases and durability
is an output.

*Compaction happens at the edges.* On-disk data is compacted when the server
starts and when it stops. Between those edges the writer only appends; a mid-life
move would let the same bytes mean two things depending on when they were read.

*One file per running prompt, forked with it.* A candidate layout — one file per
running prompt, copied on fork — fits everything above, but a copy is a snapshot,
so a reference into the original's later turns cannot be answered from it. It was
written before the world was fixed as global and unique (`research/agent-world.dj`);
if there is one record, the layout has to be re-answered.

*What the kernel already provides.* The thing under a file is the page cache, and
`mmap` with `MAP_SHARED` is the interface to it: the mapped region *is* memory,
stores are loads and stores, and the kernel is the writer. This machine has no
pmem/DAX and no hugetlbfs, and the root filesystem is btrfs, so `FICLONE` makes a
copy O(1) and copy-on-write. So the shape is `MAP_SHARED` on a btrfs file:
pointer-addressable, kernel-written, cheap to fork. The kernel offers no per-unit
integrity, so "corruption is local" is entirely our own checksums over our own
layout.

*Two spines, unresolved.* Either the mapped region *is* the memory image — the
bytes are the typed values, no decode, and no JSON/Ion/protobuf as the store —
or the file is a serialization decoded at open. Which one this is decides the
encoding, and it is not yet decided.